---
title: "Rendering"
description: "Render elements, leaves, text, overlays, voids, and hidden content with Plite React."
---

Plite React renders your document with normal React components. You provide the
components for product-specific elements, text nodes, leaves, toolbars, menus,
and overlays. Pass stable render props to `Editable` so React does not receive
a fresh renderer on every render.

## Elements

```tsx
import { Plite, Editable, useEditor } from "plitejs/react";

const ParagraphElement = ({ attributes, children }) => {
  return <p {...attributes}>{children}</p>;
};

const QuoteElement = ({ attributes, children }) => {
  return <blockquote {...attributes}>{children}</blockquote>;
};

const LinkElement = ({ attributes, children, element }) => {
  return (
    <a {...attributes} href={element.url}>
      {children}
    </a>
  );
};

const renderElement = (props) => {
  switch (props.element.type) {
    case "quote":
      return <QuoteElement {...props} />;
    case "link":
      return <LinkElement {...props} />;
    default:
      return <ParagraphElement {...props} />;
  }
};

const MyEditor = () => {
  const editor = useEditor({ initialValue });

  return (
    <Plite editor={editor}>
      <Editable renderElement={renderElement} />
    </Plite>
  );
};
```

Always spread `props.attributes` on the top-level DOM element returned by the
custom renderer, and always render `props.children`. The attributes connect the
DOM node to Plite's DOM helpers. The children render text leaves, inline
elements, void anchors, and nested editable content.

## Leaves

When text-level formatting is rendered, characters are grouped into leaves
that share the same persisted marks.

To customize leaf rendering, pass `renderLeaf`:

```tsx
const renderLeaf = ({ attributes, children, leaf }) => {
  if (leaf.bold) {
    children = <strong>{children}</strong>;
  }
  if (leaf.italic) {
    children = <em>{children}</em>;
  }

  return <span {...attributes}>{children}</span>;
};
```

Keep spreading `props.attributes` and rendering `props.children`.

When decorations split a text node into rendered segments, `renderLeaf`
receives `leafPosition`. It contains the segment's `start` and `end` offsets
plus optional `isFirst` and `isLast` flags. Decoration attributes are already
applied inside `children`; they never become fields on `leaf`.

One disadvantage of text-level formatting is that you cannot guarantee that any given format is "contiguous"—meaning that it stays as a single leaf. This limitation with respect to leaves is similar to the DOM, where this is invalid:

```markup
<em>t<strong>e</em>x</strong>t
```

Because the elements in the above example do not properly close themselves they are invalid. Instead, you would write the above HTML as follows:

```markup
<em>t</em><strong><em>e</em>x</strong>t
```

If you happened to add another overlapping section of `<strike>` to that text, you might have to rearrange the closing tags yet again. Rendering leaves in Plite is similar—you can't guarantee that even though a word has one type of formatting applied to it that that leaf will be contiguous, because it depends on how it overlaps with other formatting.

Use text-level formatting and element-level formatting for different jobs:

- Text properties are for **non-contiguous**, character-level formatting.
- Element properties are for **contiguous**, semantic elements in the document.

## Texts

`renderLeaf` customizes persisted mark presentation. Use `renderText` when you
need one wrapper around the entire text node, regardless of Decoration
boundaries.

This is where `renderText` comes in. It lets you render a component that wraps
all the leaves generated for a single `Text` node.

```tsx
const TextNode = ({ attributes, children, text }) => {
  return (
    <span {...attributes} className="custom-text" data-length={text.text.length}>
      {children}
    </span>
  );
};

<Editable renderText={TextNode} />;
```

**When to use `renderLeaf` vs `renderText`:**

- **`renderLeaf`**: Use this for persisted marks such as `leaf.bold`. It may run
  more than once for one text node when Decoration boundaries split the rendered
  content. Use `leafPosition` only when mark presentation depends on segment
  position.

- **`renderText`**: Use this when you need to render something exactly once for a given text node, regardless of how many leaves it's split into. It's ideal for wrapping the entire text node's content or adding elements associated with the text node as a whole without worrying about duplication caused by decorations.

You can use `renderText` and `renderLeaf` together. `renderLeaf` renders marks,
Decoration spans wrap transient ranges inside its children, and `renderText`
renders the container around every segment.

## Decorations

Decorations are transient range attributes. Configure ordered sources on
`<Plite decorations={sources}>`; each source returns keyed ranges with
`className`, `style`, `aria-*`, or `data-*` attributes. `Editable` applies those
attributes automatically. Decorations never become text properties or leaf
renderer inputs.

Use Decorations for search results, syntax highlighting, and external
diagnostics. Use marks for persisted text formatting. See
[Decorations, annotations, and widgets](./18-decorations-annotations-widgets)
for source observation and refresh.

## Toolbars, menus, overlays, and more!

In addition to controlling the rendering of nodes inside Plite, descendants can retrieve the provider editor with `useEditorContext()`.

Use editor-level hooks for shell UI such as toolbars. Keep broad editor subscriptions out of rendered document nodes.

A common use case for this is rendering a toolbar with formatting buttons that are highlighted based on the current selection:

```tsx
const MyEditor = () => {
  const editor = useEditor({ initialValue });

  return (
    <Plite editor={editor}>
      <Toolbar />
      <Editable />
    </Plite>
  );
};

const Toolbar = () => {
  const isBold = useEditorState((state) => {
    return state.marks()?.bold === true;
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <Button active={isBold}>B</Button>
      <Button>I</Button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

Because the toolbar draws editor-level state, `useEditorState` is the right level of subscription. It runs the selector inside `editor.read` and re-renders only when the selected result changes. Element renderers should prefer target-scoped hooks such as `useElementSelected()`, `useElementSelected({ mode: 'collapsed' })`, `useNodeSelector`, and `useTextSelector`.

## Editor styling

Custom styles can be applied to the editor itself by using the `style` prop on the `<Editable>` component.

```tsx
const MyEditor = () => {
  const editor = useEditor({ initialValue });

  return (
    <Plite editor={editor}>
      <Editable style={{ minHeight: "200px", backgroundColor: "lime" }} />
    </Plite>
  );
};
```

It is also possible to apply custom styles with a stylesheet and `className`. However, Plite uses inline styles to provide some default styles for the editor. Because inline styles take precedence over stylesheets, styles you provide using stylesheets will not override the default styles. If you are trying to use a stylesheet and your rules are not taking effect, do one of the following:

- Provide your styles using the `style` prop instead of a stylesheet, which overrides the default inline styles.
- Pass the `disableDefaultStyles` prop to the `<Editable>` component.
- Use `!important` in your stylesheet declarations to make them override the inline styles.

## Performance

See [Improving Performance](../walkthroughs/09-performance) for ways to improve the rendering performance of the editor.
